learnings:
  - When a renderer accepts an injected provider as an option (videoFrameProvider, documentResolver, etc.) and a class of layers silently doesn't render, suspect the provider was never wired by the caller. The guard 'if (provider)' is invisible at the call site — it looks like the renderer just chose not to draw.
  - Disk-loaded docs with relative-path media (image OR video) need a srcResolver — data-URL'd drag-drop assets accidentally masked this gap for images, hiding it until video forced the issue.
  - When debugging 'X looks broken' from a single screenshot, run a probe script that loads the actual doc and asserts resolveFrame's output across multiple frames before touching renderer code. The previous diagnosis path (eyeballing the screenshot) missed that the screenshot might be a cross-fade moment, not a renderer bug.
  - HTMLVideoElement.currentTime is async — drawImage(<video>) draws the LAST decoded frame, not the requested time. Provider must return null during in-flight seeks and re-invoke caller's refresh on 'seeked' event to avoid drawing stale pixels.
